WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.540
Good Morning Hank!

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:01.860
It's Wednesday, August 31st

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:04.440
Apparently it's talk to someone other than your brother week

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.020
My shirt matches the boxes behind me- I'm gonna change.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:07.940
Yeah, I think that's better, although

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.160
I wonder if we can take it down a button... nooo

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.660
So Hank I want to address the people  who have recently ended or will soon end their formal education

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:17.040
and enter what is known as the "real world"

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.520
Ok the first thing that you'll notice upon entering the real world is

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.900
that it is neither more or less real than any of the previous worlds you have encountered

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:28.600
But you'll likely notice that the real world does have this interesting mix of like, crushing monotony

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:32.100
and paralyzing terror. It's not a real world, it's more of a CMAPT world.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.320
Like, there's the crushing monotony of waking up at the same time, sitting in the same traffic, going to the same

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.860
job and then coming home to pay the same bills with your only distractions being like, television

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.320
and video games and YouTube.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.380
But there's also the constant paralyzing terror of knowing that if you somehow screw up sitting in traffic

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:50.320
or working at your job or paying your rent, you will be like, homeless and hungry and alone.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.040
This is not to worry you or anything. I'm just telling you how it feels for a lot of people. I will say that adulthood

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:55.920
gets much, much better.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.120
Speaking of which, last night my son puked on my face. Like, not on my shirt or on my shoulder or something,

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:04.940
but on my actual, like, sensory organs. Babies do this all the time, they're like little puke factories.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:10.560
To put this in context, even though a baby threw up on my face last night and I slept for, like, two hours,

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:16.560
I would still rank last night as better than almost every night of the first two years after I graduated from college

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.520
So it does get better, I promise, but I know the crushing monotony and paralyzing terror can be a little bit

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:25.300
overwhelming, plus there's like a 20% chance that you're unemployed and like, a 100% chance that you're

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:30.460
underemployed. Like, unless you're in a successful time lord rock band, you're probably not doing what you want

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:33.680
to do everyday. How are you going to solve these problems? Two ways:

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.600
Number one: Time. These things take time.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.960
Number two: You're going to get an Ilene.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:41.460
So for a few months after I graduated from college, I worked as a student chaplain at a children's hospital.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:47.120
Then I bummed around a little until I got a job at Booklist Magazine, where 90% of my job was typing ten digit

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.640
codes into a database.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.780
Kind of crushing monotony to be honest with you, but I was surrounded by books and I had these amazing

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:55.340
coworkers, most of whom were much older than I was.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.780
And they helped me to do all kinds of grownup things, like opening a checking account and not crying so much

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:03.200
when I would get dumped, which was frequently, but one of these colleagues at Booklist was a woman named

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.020
Ilene Cooper who's a writer. Ilene has published more than twenty books for children, from a picture book

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:11.420
about the golden rule to a biography of JFK to a new novel called the Angel in My Pocket that's just

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:16.360
absolutely delicious. One day I went into her office and I said, "I would like to take you out to lunch because

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:21.420
I am also a writer, and there is this book that I would like to write and it's going to be real good."

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.000
She said, "Okay", we went to the Cheesecake Factory and I was like, "I have an idea for a book.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:31.240
It's set at a boarding school and in the middle, this thing happens, and then at the end, there's an ending."

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:40.060
And Ilene said, "That sounds great." Finally, early in 2002, I brought Ilene 40 single spaced pages and for the next

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:45.260
two years, she worked with me to turn those 40 single spaced pages into a novel that would eventually become

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:46.320
Looking For Alaska.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.840
The only reason Looking For Alaska exists, the only reason I have a job writing books, the only reason there's

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:54.780
Brotherhood 2.0 or Nightfighteria is Ilene. Whoever you are, to get through the period of crushing monotony and

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:59.660
paralyzing terror, you need an Ilene. And it can't be me because for one thing, I'm already trying to be an Ilene

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:03.240
for a bunch of people, like Ransom Riggs, author of Miss Peregrine's Home For Peculiar Children,

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:07.660
I was kind of a little bit of an Ilene for him. Not as much of an Ilene as Ilene is, but a little.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:11.340
So that's my advice for navigating the treacherous waters of life after school: Find someone who's doing

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:15.700
something you want to do better than you can do it, and listen to them. And you should really check out

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:20.020
Ilene's book Angel in My Pocket for the middle grade reader in your life. There is a link in the dooblydoo.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:27.920
It's great. Not the link, the book. The link is just a regular link. I have signed 42,000 copies of your books and I am

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:33.520
going to continue but first I have to go the doctor because my hand isn't working right, and I've got to get

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:35.880
occupational therapy. I wish I were making that up.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.716
Hank, I will see you on Friday.

